A||||5||AkursedX||A/C  Belt Replacement||||13||
Z||000000||AkursedX||08-20-2000||02:17 PM||||Well, yesterday I took on the task of replacing the A/C belt on my 88 GT.  This was by ar the toughest thing I've had to do on my car!  I couldn't believe how tough it was.  <P>I do have a question though.  For people who have replaced this, did you go through the top or the bottom to loosen the one bolt that is more or less above the oil filter so you can move the A/C Compressor?<BR>I went through the bottom, and I used about a 3ft extension with a swivel end at the socket and managed to get the socket on and the long extension gave me enough room to turn the wrench.  It's a good thing I work out 5 days a week because that bolt was so frozen that I thought I snapped the wrench.  But fortunately I got it loosened, and now my A/C belt doesn't squeal   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>So anyways, I was just wondering if it would of been any easier to remove the decklid and go through the top.<P>And to all who have to replace this, good luck, and to all who have already, I feel for ya, lol.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000001||Getaway2k||08-20-2000||02:41 PM||||I was going to replace the a/c belt on my 86 GT last winter (nothing wrong with it, just preventative maintenance), but once I got under there and saw where the bolts were, I took the belt back for a refund!!! If you've got the time I'd appreciate you detailing what you went through to change it.<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-20-2000||03:03 PM||||I have been trying to put a belt on the compressor of my 4cyl, but can't seem to get the damn thing to move! I have loosened all the bolts I can find, but it will not budge. It doesn't feel like it is frozen in place, but there is still some bolts to be loosened? I can't figure this out. Any help would be appreciated! Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||AkursedX||08-20-2000||03:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Getaway2k:<BR><B>If you've got the time I'd appreciate you detailing what you went through to change it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, well I do got alot of time today, lol.  <BR>-Ok well I jacked up the car (REAL HIGH) and put it on stands<BR>-Take of passenger side wheel<BR>-Take off all the plastic wheel-well stuff.  The little screw things are a pain in the butt, I broke a couple of them.<BR>-then you should be able to see the main pulley.  Well you have to take off the main belt to get to the a/c belt.<BR>-I know theres 2 bolts that you got to loosen on the alternator.  They are both 13mm I believe.  I think you take out the one on the bottom completely.  And then you gotta go to the top of the car (I stood on the tire to reach) and there is a bolt attached to the alternator right by the heat shield to the header that you have to loosen.  <BR>-Then you should be able to wiggle the alternator and loosen it to remove the belt.<BR>-now to the A/c compressor.  Again, I think there is a bolt on the front o the compressor that you have to take out 15mm I think. I'm not sure about this because my dad did this part.  <BR>-Then, this is the fun one.  Crawl underneath in front of the drivers side wheel.  Now with a flashlight you should be able to see the bracket that holds the A/C compressor up.  I think there are 3 bolts that hold it on, but the one you need to loosen is the top one.  It kinda forms an apex at the top, and it's very difficult to see.  <BR>-Get a 15mm socket and you will definietly need a swivel thing with the socket to get it on.  Then I used my 2 longest extensions and put them together, it was about 3 feet long.  Now with this and a little bit of time, you should be able to get the socket on the bolt and manage to loosen it.  Now mine belt was never change, and that bolt was very diffcult to get that first turn.<BR>-After you get that loosened, you should be able to move the compressor around.  I had to use a prybar, but i got it to move.  <BR>-Then you should be able to take of the old belt and put the new one on.<BR>-Putting everything together is basically the opposite of taking it apart.  Just make sure the belts are snug, but not too snug.<P>Wow that was alot of typing   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Hope I am of assistance to some people out there.<P>I apologize for any mis-spells, but I'm too lazt to go and look back through it, lol.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-22-2000||10:17 AM||||When I change the A/C belt in my 85SE v-6 I only loosen the lower adjusting bolt on the A/C bracket.  There is no need to loosen the top mounted bolts (the ones the bracket swivels from).  I find that removing the small heat shield under the A/C bracket (three sheet metal hex head screws - 10mm I think) makes it easier to get a wrench on the bolts and to insert a large screwdriver in the adjusting slots to lever the tension into the belt.<P>I do remove the right rear wheel and the inner wheel well and curtain and work from underneath the car.<P>When I installed my new motor I used the belt tensioning device I got from JC Whitney.  It works great.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LAMBO||08-22-2000||01:35 PM||||I second everthing that carrolles said. I've put on two AC belts in the last two weeks. I didn't remove the wheel or wheel well stuff though. Didn't need to, I guess. Yeah, its a pain in the arse, but we're all having fun, right! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
